      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "The \\( y \\)-intercept for the line \\( y = ax + b \\) is found by observing the point where \\( x = 0 \\). The ordered \\( y \\)-intercept of the line, \\( b \\), when \\( x = 0 \\) is our answer to this task. This is calculated as \\( b = y \\) when \\( x = 0 \\), because our given set of points is given in the form of \\( (x, y) \\).\n\nWe know that the line \\( y = ax + b \\) has a \\( y \\)-intercept of \\( b \\) and it also passes through the points \\((4, 5)\\) and \\((8, 17)\\). Using these points, we find \\( a \\) and \\( b \\).\n\nCalculating \\( a \\) from point sets:\n\\[ b = \\text{y coordinate at the x-intercept}(y = 0) \\]\n\\[ 5 = a \\cdot 4 + b \\text{, and } 17 = a \\cdot 8 + b \\]\n\nSubtract the first equation from the second to eliminate \\( b \\):\n\\[ 17 - 5 = a \\cdot 8 - a \\cdot 4 \\]\n\\[ 9 = 4a \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{9}{4} \\]\n\nNext, substituting the found \\( a \\) into one of the original forms to solve for \\( b \\):\n\\[ 5 = 4 \\cdot 4 + b \\]\n\\[ 5 = 16 + b \\]\n\\[ b = 5 - 16 \\]\n\\[ b = -11 \\]\n\nFinally, the difference \\( a - b \\) is:\n\\[ a - b = \\frac{9}{4} - (-11) \\]\n\\[ a - b = \\frac{9}{4} + 11 \\]\n\\[ a - b = \\frac{9}{4} + \\frac{44}{4} \\]\n\\[ a - b = \\frac{9 + 44}{4} \\]\n\\[ a - b = \\frac{53}{4} \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{53}{4}} \\]",
